# Where the DisjointMut borrow tracker's time actually goes (recon, not a fix)

date: 2026-08-07
host: Apple M4 Pro (8 P-core + 4 E-core = 12 logical), macOS 26.5.2, aarch64
rustc: stable, release profile (lto = "fat", codegen-units = 1), default
       features (bitdepth_8 + bitdepth_16 — the CHECKED DisjointMut build).
       No `-C target-cpu=native`.
base commit: a6a7e23 (main — #445 as merged; NOT the #450 batching branch)
probe apparatus: commit 1d7063d on branch `recon/tracker-profile`

## nice level — read this before reproducing

NO `nice` WAS APPLIED TO ANY TIMED RUN. On Darwin a positive nice value maps the
process to background QoS (efficiency cores) and distorts wall clock by well
over an order of magnitude. `nice -n 19` was used for BUILDS only.

Arms are interleaved back to back per (vector, threads) cell and their order is
rotated each round. Every number below is a MEDIAN OF 9 (3 rounds x 3 reps).
`scripts/perf/ab_sweep.sh` refuses to start while any process is above 25% CPU
and records the 1-minute load with every cell; observed load 1.60 / 2.45 / 3.70
(min / median / max) across 336 cells, zero FAIL rows.

Box-sharing note: a sibling agent was running its own timed sweep in
`~/tmp/recon-yard` for part of this session. Nothing here overlaps it — the
decomposition sweep started only after that sweep wrote its output. A third
contaminator showed up unprompted: `corespotlightd` hit 95% CPU indexing the
build trees under `~/tmp` and paused both agents' harnesses. `~/tmp/rav1d-recon`
and `~/tmp/rav1d-perf` now carry a `.metadata_never_index` marker.

## The four arms

Builds of `examples/bench_ab_decode.rs` that differ ONLY in how much of
`BorrowTracker` is present (features in this branch's `Cargo.toml`):

  base       everything — this is what main ships
  noscan     lock + slot bookkeeping, `find_overlap_*` removed
  lockonly   lock/unlock only; no scan, no slot bookkeeping
  untracked  no tracker at all (`DisjointMut::new` builds an untracked instance)

`untracked` is unsound by construction. It exists to measure the ceiling, and
`unchecked` was deliberately NOT used for it: `unchecked` also turns on
`get_unchecked` in hot paths, which would conflate two effects.

All four arms produce byte-identical decoded frames — every cell, every thread
count, `md5 all-agree`, and no arm's output varies with thread count.

## Arm validity

`base` was built before the probe source was restructured, so it carries a
shared `add_probed` body instead of main's textual `add_mut`. It was checked
against a build of pristine `main` (`git archive main`, same harness):
24 of 28 cells within +-2%. The four exceptions are all v4k_8tile at t>=4 — the
anti-scaling convoy cells, which are intrinsically noisy: 8bpc t=8 spans
1264-1343 ms across builds (+-3%), 10bpc t=8 spans 1303-1659 ms (+-13%). So the
"tracker share at t=8" headline carries roughly +-3 points, and its direction is
not in question. Since 1d7063d a default build's tracker source is textually
identical to main.

## Result 1 — the ceiling. Without the tracker, tile threading works.

ms/frame, median of 9, 3840x2160 8bpc 8-tile (`x` = speedup vs that arm's t=1):

  arm         t=1     t=2           t=4           t=8
  base        586.5   496.3 (1.18x) 668.3 (0.88x) 1264.4 (0.46x)
  noscan      599.2   515.3 (1.16x) 686.9 (0.87x) 1080.6 (0.55x)
  lockonly    504.0   351.5 (1.43x) 307.6 (1.64x)  402.5 (1.25x)
  untracked   462.2   287.5 (1.61x) 200.1 (2.31x)  147.5 (3.13x)

Same shape at 10bpc: untracked 547.2 / 342.1 / 245.0 / 181.1 (3.02x at t=8).

* THE CEILING IS 147 ms/frame AT t=8, and scaling to it is monotone: 1.61x,
  2.31x, 3.13x. Anti-scaling is not intrinsic to the decoder's tile threading —
  it is the tracker.
* The best configuration main can actually ship today is t=2 at 496 ms. The
  ceiling is 3.4x better than that, and 8.6x better than main at t=8.
* 3.13x from 8 threads is itself sub-linear on 8 P-cores. That residual (tile
  imbalance, the serial non-tile work, memory bandwidth) is the real ceiling,
  not 8x. Chasing past ~3x on this content is chasing something else.
* `lockonly` — take the lock, do nothing, release it — already destroys most of
  the scaling: 402.5 ms vs untracked's 147.5 ms at t=8, i.e. 2.7x. AN EMPTY
  CRITICAL SECTION ON A SHARED LINE COSTS 2.7x AT t=8. Any design that keeps one
  atomic RMW per borrow on a line shared between tile workers cannot reach the
  ceiling, no matter how cheap the check inside becomes.

## Result 2 — lock vs scan: it is NEITHER. It is the slot bookkeeping.

Differences of medians, ms/frame. `scan = base - noscan`,
`bookkeep = noscan - lockonly`, `lock = lockonly - untracked`.

  vector          t  base    untracked  tracker  tracker%  scan    bookkeep  lock
  v4k_1tile 8bpc  1  585.4   462.6      122.8    21.0      -16.5    97.7     41.6
  v4k_1tile 8bpc  8  584.0   404.5      179.5    30.7      -13.3   140.1     52.6
  v4k_8tile 8bpc  1  586.5   462.2      124.3    21.2      -12.7    95.2     41.8
  v4k_8tile 8bpc  2  496.3   287.5      208.7    42.1      -19.0   163.8     63.9
  v4k_8tile 8bpc  4  668.3   200.1      468.2    70.1      -18.7   379.4    107.5
  v4k_8tile 8bpc  8 1264.4   147.5     1116.9    88.3      183.8   678.1    255.1
  v1024     8bpc  1   52.1    40.7       11.4    21.8       -1.4     9.2      3.6
  v256      8bpc  1    1.19    0.97       0.22   18.2       -0.02    0.16     0.07

* At t=1, where contention is exactly zero (measured: 0 contended acquisitions),
  the split is unambiguous: raw lock traffic 41.6 ms, slot bookkeeping 97.7 ms,
  overlap scan not measurable. THE SCAN IS FREE AND THE LOCK IS THE MINORITY
  SHARE; the cost is the per-borrow record keeping (free-slot search, four
  parallel-array stores, the `Location` store, the BorrowId round trip).
* `scan` comes out NEGATIVE at every thread count below 8 — `noscan` is
  1-3% SLOWER than `base`. That is codegen jitter around zero, not a real
  negative cost. Read it as "the scan costs nothing measurable", which Result 3
  explains: the table it scans is essentially always empty.
* At t=8 the components stop being independent. Anything that lengthens the
  critical section multiplies everyone else's waiting, so the 183.8 / 678.1 /
  255.1 split at t=8 is "wall time recovered by removing that piece", not "wall
  time spent inside that piece". The scan only starts to cost at t=8 because of
  what it does to hold time, not because the loop got longer.
* The tracker is 18-22% of decode at t=1 on EVERY size from 256x144 to 4K. It is
  not a large-image problem; it is a per-borrow problem.

## Result 3 — the numbers behind that: ~50 MILLION borrows per 4K frame

From the `probe-count` build (`examples/probe_tracker.rs`), 3840x2160 4:4:4:

  threads  adds/frame   lock ops/frame  contended  mean wait/contended  spills
  1        47,219,427    94,438,854     0.000%       —                  0
  2        50,776,895   101,553,790     0.747%      52.8 ns             0
  4        50,776,895   101,553,790     3.312%     305.6 ns             0
  8        50,776,895   101,553,790     5.046%     950.7 ns             0

* FIFTY MILLION borrow registrations per frame, each costing two lock round
  trips (register + guard drop) = ~101.5 million acquisitions per frame. At
  t=1's measured 122.8 ms of tracker cost that is ~1.3 ns per lock op — the
  tracker is already about as cheap per operation as a hot-in-L1 atomic can be.
  THE VOLUME IS THE PROBLEM, NOT THE PER-OP COST.
* The count does NOT change with thread count. t=2, t=4 and t=8 are identical to
  the borrow. Only contention changes. (t=1 is 7.5% lower because #445's compact
  per-row path is gated on `tile_threading_active()`, which is false at n_tc=1.)
* Per thread at t=8 the work is spread evenly: 19.3M-31.6M adds each over 4
  frames, i.e. ~6.3M borrows per thread per frame.
* Contention RATE saturates around 5% but the COST PER CONTENDED ACQUISITION
  grows 18x from t=2 to t=8 (52.8 -> 950.7 ns). That is a convoy, not a
  collision: waiters queue behind each other on one line. Aggregate spin time is
  4.87 seconds per frame across 8 threads (24% of aggregate thread time in the
  instrumented build).
* Single-tile 4K is the control: contention stays flat at 0.31-0.40% and ~53 ns
  from t=2 to t=8, and decode time stays flat. Nothing escalates when the
  workers are not sharing planes.

The probe updates its counters OUTSIDE the critical section, so it cannot
inflate the hold time it measures. It does lengthen the whole decode (2558 ms vs
1264 ms at t=8), which DEFLATES the contention percentage — the real rate is
higher than 5.05%, not lower.

## Result 4 — the 64 inline slots are ~100x oversized, and never spill

Per-instance, at every thread count and every vector measured:

  mean occupancy at registration time   0.01 (t=1) .. 0.34 (t=8) slots of 64
  max occupancy ever observed           7 at t=4, 8 at t=8
  overflow spills to the heap Vec       0, always

* The table holds at most one live interval per worker thread (plus a little
  nesting). It is scanned ~0.2 entries deep on average. That is why the scan
  measures free — and it means the ENTIRE 64-slot design is solving a problem
  that does not exist.
* `BorrowSlots` is ~2.6 KB of PARALLEL arrays (starts / ends / mutable / locs).
  Every single registration writes four of them, so one borrow dirties up to
  four distinct cache lines — all of them shared between every tile worker. That
  is the mechanism behind Result 2's "bookkeeping" term and behind `lockonly`
  still costing 2.7x at t=8.

## Result 5 — the contention is on TWELVE trackers, each shared by every worker

Per-instance attribution, v4k_8tile 8bpc, t=8, 1039 tracker instances live,
zero probe-slot exhaustion (`tracker_counts_bigslots_2026-08-07.txt`):

  12 instances with max_end = 8,355,840 B (a 3840x2176 4:4:4 plane)
     -> 89.8% of ALL borrows in the process
     -> 100% of all contention (6.96-8.77% contended on the busiest four,
        3.44-4.77% on the other eight)
     -> every one of them touched by all 8 threads
  the other 1,027 instances -> 10.2% of borrows, ZERO contended acquisitions

Twelve = 4 in-flight pictures x 3 planes; `Rav1dPictureDataComponent` holds one
`DisjointMut<PicBuf>` per plane, so every tile of a frame funnels through the
same three locks.

* It is not one lock, but the distinction that matters is the other one: each of
  the twelve is shared by EVERY tile worker. Sharding the tracker across
  instances therefore buys nothing — the sharding has to be WITHIN an instance,
  and the natural axis is the one the callers already have (tile / thread /
  row band).
* Nothing else is contended at all. A fix scoped to picture planes would capture
  essentially the whole win.

## Result 6 — `sample` agrees, at t=8

Flat top-of-stack profile, 12 s of v4k_8tile at t=8, `base` arm
(`tracker_sample_t8_flat_2026-08-07.txt`; `__psynch_cvwait` = idle workers,
excluded from the denominator of ~33k non-idle samples):

  TinyLock::lock_slow              12,329   ~37%
  BorrowTracker::add_*              8,414   ~26%
  DisjointMutGuard drop glue        1,468    ~4%
  ---------------------------------------------
  tracker total                    22,211   ~67% of non-idle CPU samples
  decode_coefs                      3,460   ~10%
  cdef_direct                       1,690    ~5%

The earlier `sample`-derived figure of "add_mut ~13% of decode" was measured at
low thread counts. At t=8 the tracker is two thirds of all non-idle CPU time and
pure spinning alone outweighs the hottest real decode kernel by 3.6x.

## What this says for the redesign (no design decisions taken here)

1. The target is 147 ms/frame at t=8 on v4k_8tile 8bpc. That is measured, not
   projected, and it is 3.4x better than the best thread count main can ship.
2. Do not optimise the overlap scan. It is free (Result 2) because the table it
   scans is empty (Result 3/4).
3. Reducing contention is not sufficient. `lockonly` has a completely empty
   critical section and still costs 2.7x at t=8 — the shared cache line is the
   cost. The common-case borrow has to stop touching memory shared with other
   tile workers.
4. Reducing the borrow COUNT is the other lever, and it is large: 50M/frame.
5. Whatever replaces it must still catch real overlaps (`unchecked` already
   exists for callers who opt out) and must not add unsafe outside
   rav1d-disjoint-mut.

## Reproducing

  # vectors: see benchmarks/tile_threading_guard_2026-08-06.meta
  for f in "" probe-noscan probe-lockonly probe-untracked; do
    CARGO_TARGET_DIR=... RUSTC_WRAPPER=sccache nice -n 19 \
      cargo build --release --example bench_ab_decode ${f:+--features $f} -j 4
  done
  caffeinate -i ./scripts/perf/ab_sweep.sh <vecdir> out.tsv 3 \
    base=... noscan=... lockonly=... untracked=...
  python3 scripts/perf/tracker_decomp.py out.tsv

  cargo build --release --example probe_tracker --features probe-count -j 4
  ./probe_tracker <vec>/v4k_8tile.avif 8 4

